$(
=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
  Square matrices
=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

  In the following, the square matrix algebra is defined as extensible
  structure ` Mat ` .  In this subsection, however, only square matrices and
  their basic properties are regarded.  This includes showing that
  ` ( N Mat R ) ` is a left module, see ~ matlmod .  That ` ( N Mat R ) ` is a
  ring and an associative algebra is shown in the next subsection, after
  theorems about the identity matrix are available.  Nevertheless,
  ` ( N Mat R ) ` is called "matrix ring" or "matrix algebra" already in this
  subsection.

$)

  $c Mat $.

  $( Syntax for the square matrix algebra. $)
  cmat $a class Mat $.

  ${
    $d n r $.
    $( Define the algebra of n x n matrices over a ring r.  (Contributed by
       Stefan O'Rear, 31-Aug-2015.) $)
    df-mat $a |- Mat = ( n e. Fin , r e. _V |-> ( ( r freeLMod ( n X. n ) )
        sSet <. ( .r ` ndx ) , ( r maMul <. n , n , n >. ) >. ) ) $.
  $}

